GENERAL KILLERCODA INSTRUCTIONS
===============================
1. Open https://killercoda.com/
2. Sign in if required.
3. Search for Docker or Kubernetes playground/scenarios.
4. Start the relevant scenario.
5. Wait until the terminal is ready.
6. Type or paste the commands in that scenario's terminal.
7. Docker and Kubernetes scenarios may be separate environments.
   Files created in one scenario may NOT exist in another.
8. If a scenario expires or resets, start it again and recreate the files.

============================================================
PRACTICAL 1 - DOCKER WITH PYTHON FLASK
============================================================

AIM:
Create a local Python Flask application, build a Docker image,
run a container and test the application.

WHERE TO DO:
Killercoda Docker playground terminal.
If Docker commands fail because Docker Engine is unavailable, use
VS Code terminal on Windows with Docker Desktop running.

STEP 1 - CHECK DOCKER
--------------------
docker --version
docker info

If `docker info` reports that it cannot connect to the daemon,
the selected environment does not have a working Docker daemon.
Use a Docker-enabled Killercoda scenario or Docker Desktop.

STEP 2 - CREATE PROJECT FOLDER
------------------------------
mkdir -p docker-flask
cd docker-flask

STEP 3 - CREATE app.py
----------------------
Run this complete command in the terminal:

cat > app.py <<'EOF'
from flask import Flask, jsonify

app = Flask(__name__)

students = [
    {"id": 1, "name": "Roji", "branch": "CSE"},
    {"id": 2, "name": "Akshaya", "branch": "CSE"},
    {"id": 3, "name": "Divya", "branch": "CSE"}
]

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return "Welcome to Flask Docker Application!"

@app.route("/students")
def get_students():
    return jsonify(students)

@app.route("/health")
def health():
    return jsonify({"status": "Application is running"})

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)
EOF

STEP 4 - CREATE requirements.txt
--------------------------------
cat > requirements.txt <<'EOF'
Flask==3.1.2
EOF

STEP 5 - CREATE Dockerfile
--------------------------
The filename must be exactly Dockerfile, with no extension.

cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
EOF

STEP 6 - CREATE .dockerignore
-----------------------------
cat > .dockerignore <<'EOF'
__pycache__/
*.pyc
venv/
.env
.git/
EOF

STEP 7 - CHECK FILES
--------------------
ls -la

You should see:
app.py
requirements.txt
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

STEP 8 - BUILD THE IMAGE
------------------------
docker build -t student-app .

The dot at the end is important. It means the current directory
is the Docker build context.

STEP 9 - CHECK THE IMAGE
------------------------
docker images

You should see an image named student-app.

STEP 10 - RUN THE CONTAINER
---------------------------
docker run -d -p 5001:5000 --name student-container student-app

STEP 11 - CHECK THE CONTAINER
-----------------------------
docker ps

STEP 12 - TEST THE APPLICATION
------------------------------
curl http://localhost:5001
curl http://localhost:5001/students
curl http://localhost:5001/health

Expected responses:
Home:
Welcome to Flask Docker Application!

Students:
A JSON array containing three student records.

Health:
{"status":"Application is running"}

If the Killercoda scenario provides a port preview/open-port feature,
open port 5001 in that feature to view the web page.

STEP 13 - CONTAINER MANAGEMENT
------------------------------
docker logs student-container
docker stop student-container
docker start student-container
docker restart student-container
docker rm -f student-container

To remove the image after removing the container:
docker rmi student-app

NOTE:
If you remove the container before checking logs, logs will no
longer be available. Check logs first.

============================================================
PRACTICAL 2 - KUBERNETES CONTAINER ORCHESTRATION
============================================================

AIM:
Deploy an application using Kubernetes and manage Pods,
Deployments and Services.

WHERE TO DO:
Killercoda Kubernetes playground terminal.
Start a Kubernetes scenario that provides a working cluster.
Do not run these commands in a Docker-only playground.

STEP 1 - CHECK KUBECTL AND CLUSTER
----------------------------------
kubectl version --client
kubectl get nodes

Wait until the node status is Ready.
If `kubectl get nodes` fails, the Kubernetes cluster is not ready
or the scenario is not a Kubernetes environment.

STEP 2 - CREATE PROJECT FOLDER
------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/kubernetes-practical
cd ~/kubernetes-practical

STEP 3 - CREATE deployment.yaml
-------------------------------
cat > deployment.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: student-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: student
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: student
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: student-container
          image: nginx:latest
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
EOF

STEP 4 - CREATE THE DEPLOYMENT
------------------------------
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Expected:
deployment.apps/student-deployment created

STEP 5 - CHECK DEPLOYMENT
-------------------------
kubectl get deployments
kubectl describe deployment student-deployment

STEP 6 - CHECK PODS
-------------------
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods -o wide

Wait until all three Pods show Running and Ready.

STEP 7 - SCALE THE DEPLOYMENT
-----------------------------
kubectl scale deployment student-deployment --replicas=5

Check:
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods

There should now be five Pods.

STEP 8 - EXPOSE THE DEPLOYMENT
------------------------------
kubectl expose deployment student-deployment --type=NodePort --port=80

Check the Service:
kubectl get services
kubectl describe service student-deployment

STEP 9 - TEST THE SERVICE
-------------------------
The exact way to open a NodePort depends on the Killercoda scenario.
First inspect the service and note its NodePort:

kubectl get service student-deployment

A simple test from inside the cluster is:

kubectl run curl-test --image=curlimages/curl:latest --restart=Never -it --rm -- sh

At the prompt inside the temporary test Pod, run:
curl http://student-deployment

Then type:
exit

If the interactive command does not work in the scenario, use:
kubectl run curl-test --image=curlimages/curl:latest --restart=Never --command -- sleep 300
kubectl exec curl-test -- curl http://student-deployment
kubectl delete pod curl-test

Expected:
Nginx welcome page HTML.

STEP 10 - VIEW EVENTS
---------------------
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

STEP 11 - DELETE RESOURCES
--------------------------
kubectl delete service student-deployment
kubectl delete deployment student-deployment

Verify:
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods
kubectl get services

============================================================
PRACTICAL 3 - KUBERNETES CONFIGMAP
============================================================

AIM:
Create a ConfigMap and use its values in a Kubernetes application
as environment variables and as a mounted file.

WHERE TO DO:
Killercoda Kubernetes playground terminal.
Use the Kubernetes cluster from Practical 2 if it is still active.
If it has reset, start a new Kubernetes scenario and check:
kubectl get nodes

STEP 1 - CREATE PROJECT FOLDER
------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/configmap-practical
cd ~/configmap-practical

STEP 2 - CREATE configmap.yaml
------------------------------
cat > configmap.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: student-config
data:
  APP_NAME: "Student Management System"
  APP_ENV: "Development"
  APP_VERSION: "1.0"
  WELCOME_MESSAGE: "Welcome to Kubernetes ConfigMap"
EOF

STEP 3 - CREATE THE CONFIGMAP
-----------------------------
kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml

STEP 4 - VERIFY CONFIGMAP
-------------------------
kubectl get configmaps
kubectl describe configmap student-config
kubectl get configmap student-config -o yaml

STEP 5 - CREATE deployment.yaml
-------------------------------
cat > deployment.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: configmap-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: configmap-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: configmap-app
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx-container
          image: nginx:latest
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: student-config
          volumeMounts:
            - name: config-volume
              mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
              readOnly: true
      volumes:
        - name: config-volume
          configMap:
            name: student-config
            items:
              - key: WELCOME_MESSAGE
                path: index.html
EOF

STEP 6 - DEPLOY APPLICATION
---------------------------
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

STEP 7 - CHECK DEPLOYMENT AND PODS
----------------------------------
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods

Wait until both Pods show Running and Ready.

STEP 8 - CHECK ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE
-----------------------------------
kubectl get pods

Copy one Pod name from the output. Replace POD_NAME below
with the actual name (do not type the angle brackets):

kubectl exec POD_NAME -- printenv APP_NAME

Expected:
Student Management System

Check another variable:
kubectl exec POD_NAME -- printenv APP_ENV

Expected:
Development

STEP 9 - TEST THE MOUNTED CONFIGURATION
---------------------------------------
Run port forwarding:

kubectl port-forward deployment/configmap-deployment 8080:80

Keep this command running in this terminal.
Open a second terminal in the same Killercoda scenario and run:

curl http://localhost:8080

Expected:
Welcome to Kubernetes ConfigMap

If the scenario offers a port preview, open port 8080 there.

Stop port forwarding with Ctrl+C when finished.

STEP 10 - UPDATE THE CONFIGMAP
------------------------------
Edit configmap.yaml and change the WELCOME_MESSAGE value to:

WELCOME_MESSAGE: "Updated Configuration Successfully"

Apply the updated ConfigMap:

kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml

Restart the Deployment:

kubectl rollout restart deployment configmap-deployment

Wait for rollout:

kubectl rollout status deployment configmap-deployment

Check Pods:

kubectl get pods

Test again using port-forward in one terminal:
kubectl port-forward deployment/configmap-deployment 8080:80

In a second terminal:
curl http://localhost:8080

Expected:
Updated Configuration Successfully

IMPORTANT:
ConfigMap values mounted as files update eventually, but restarting
the Deployment makes the test predictable. ConfigMap values injected
as environment variables do not change inside already-running
containers; restart is required for new values to be loaded.

STEP 11 - DELETE RESOURCES
--------------------------
kubectl delete deployment configmap-deployment
kubectl delete configmap student-config

Verify:
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get configmaps

============================================================
PRACTICAL 4 - GITHUB BASICS
============================================================

AIM:
Perform basic Git and GitHub operations: initialize a repository,
track files, stage, commit, create a remote repository, push,
branch, pull and clone.

WHERE TO DO:
Use the Killercoda terminal if Git is installed.
Otherwise use VS Code terminal or Windows PowerShell.
Use a browser to create the GitHub repository.

STEP 1 - CHECK GIT
------------------
git --version

If Git is not installed in Killercoda, use VS Code/PowerShell
or a Git-enabled scenario.

STEP 2 - CONFIGURE GIT
----------------------
Replace the sample details with your own GitHub name and email:

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

Check:
git config --global --list

STEP 3 - CREATE PROJECT
-----------------------
mkdir -p ~/github-basics
cd ~/github-basics

STEP 4 - CREATE app.py
----------------------
cat > app.py <<'EOF'
print("Welcome to GitHub Basics Practical")

student_name = "Roji"
branch = "CSE"

print("Student Name:", student_name)
print("Branch:", branch)

print("GitHub operations completed successfully!")
EOF

STEP 5 - CREATE README.md
-------------------------
cat > README.md <<'EOF'
# GitHub Basics Practical

## Aim
To understand basic Git and GitHub operations.

## Description
This project demonstrates Git initialization,
file tracking, staging, committing and pushing
code to a GitHub repository.

## Technologies Used
- Python
- Git
- GitHub

## Execution
python app.py
EOF

STEP 6 - RUN THE PYTHON PROGRAM (OPTIONAL)
------------------------------------------
python3 app.py

If Python is not installed in the selected terminal,
skip this optional test. Git operations do not require Python.

STEP 7 - INITIALIZE GIT
-----------------------
git init

STEP 8 - CHECK STATUS
---------------------
git status

STEP 9 - STAGE FILES
--------------------
git add .

Check:
git status

STEP 10 - COMMIT FILES
----------------------
git commit -m "Initial commit"

STEP 11 - CREATE GITHUB REPOSITORY IN BROWSER
---------------------------------------------
1. Open https://github.com/
2. Sign in to your GitHub account.
3. Click the + icon and choose New repository.
4. Enter repository name: github-basics
5. Select Public or Private.
6. Do NOT select Add a README file because README.md
   has already been created locally.
7. Click Create repository.
8. Copy the HTTPS repository URL.

It will look like:
https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/github-basics.git

STEP 12 - CONNECT LOCAL REPOSITORY
----------------------------------
Rename the current branch:
git branch -M main

Add the remote. Replace YOUR_USERNAME:
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/github-basics.git

Check:
git remote -v

STEP 13 - PUSH TO GITHUB
------------------------
git push -u origin main

If GitHub asks you to authenticate, follow the browser sign-in
or token process offered by Git. A normal GitHub account password
is not accepted for HTTPS Git pushes.

STEP 14 - ADD ANOTHER FILE AND PUSH AGAIN
-----------------------------------------
Create a file:

cat > notes.txt <<'EOF'
This file is added to demonstrate another Git commit.
EOF

Stage and commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added notes file"

Push:
git push

STEP 15 - VIEW COMMIT HISTORY
-----------------------------
git log --oneline

STEP 16 - CREATE AND SWITCH BRANCH
----------------------------------
git branch feature
git switch feature

Create a change:
echo "Feature branch practice" > feature.txt

Stage and commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added feature branch file"

Switch back:
git switch main

STEP 17 - PULL LATEST CHANGES
-----------------------------
git pull origin main

STEP 18 - CLONE REPOSITORY
--------------------------
Move to a different directory:
cd ~

Clone (replace YOUR_USERNAME):
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/github-basics.git

Enter cloned project:
cd github-basics

List files:
ls

NOTE:
If you created a feature branch locally but did not push it,
it will not appear on GitHub. To push it, use:
git push -u origin feature

============================================================
COMMON ERRORS AND SOLUTIONS
============================================================

1. Docker: Cannot connect to the Docker daemon
   Solution: You are probably in a terminal without Docker Engine.
   Start a Docker-enabled Killercoda scenario or use Docker Desktop.

2. Docker: port is already allocated
   Solution: Choose another host port, for example:
   docker run -d -p 5002:5000 --name student-container student-app
   Then test http://localhost:5002.

3. Kubernetes: kubectl get nodes fails
   Solution: Start a Kubernetes Killercoda scenario and wait
   until its cluster is ready.

4. Kubernetes: ImagePullBackOff
   Solution: Check internet/image access and inspect:
   kubectl describe pod POD_NAME

5. Kubernetes: Pod is not Running immediately
   Solution: Wait and check:
   kubectl get pods
   kubectl describe pod POD_NAME

6. ConfigMap not found
   Solution: Create the ConfigMap before applying the Deployment:
   kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml
   kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

7. Git: remote origin already exists
   Solution:
   git remote -v
   git remote set-url origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/github-basics.git

8. Git: src refspec main does not match any
   Solution: Make sure at least one commit exists:
   git add .
   git commit -m "Initial commit"
   git branch -M main
   git push -u origin main

9. GitHub authentication failed
   Solution: Use Git Credential Manager/browser sign-in or a
   properly configured Personal Access Token. Never share tokens.

